{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Code Quality Reviewer",
  "description": "Analyzes modified source code files for potential improvements including code smells, design patterns, and best practices. Provides suggestions for readability, maintainability, and performance optimizations.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "fileEdited",
    "patterns": [
      "stocker/lib/**/*.dart",
      "stocker-backend-node/src/**/*.js",
      "stocker-backend-node/scripts/**/*.js"
    ]
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "Analyze the edited file for code quality improvements. Review the code and provide concise, actionable suggestions covering:\n\n1. Code smells: Identify duplicated logic, overly long methods, deep nesting, magic numbers/strings, and unused variables or imports.\n2. Design patterns: Suggest applicable patterns (e.g., repository pattern, singleton, factory, observer) where they would improve structure. For Dart/Flutter files, check for proper state management, widget decomposition, and separation of concerns. For Node.js files, check for proper MVC separation, middleware usage, and error handling patterns.\n3. Best practices: Check adherence to language-specific conventions (Dart effective style / Node.js best practices), proper error handling, null safety (Dart), async/await usage, and consistent naming conventions.\n4. Readability: Flag unclear variable/function names, missing or misleading comments, and overly complex expressions that could be simplified.\n5. Maintainability: Identify tightly coupled code, missing abstractions, and opportunities to extract reusable utilities or constants.\n6. Performance: Spot unnecessary rebuilds (Flutter), redundant API calls, inefficient data structures, missing database indexes or query optimizations (Node.js), and memory leak risks.\n\nKeep suggestions practical and focused. Do not rewrite the entire file — just highlight the top issues with brief explanations and recommended fixes. Preserve existing functionality."
  }
}